# pet.hal — a tiny terminal creature that keeps living between runs.
# Run it from the folder where it should live:
#   hal -d examples/pet.hal            say hello
#   hal -d examples/pet.hal feed       feed it
#   hal -d examples/pet.hal play       play with it
#   hal -d examples/pet.hal sleep      put it to bed

The pet lives in pet.json in the folder I run this from. If there is no
pet.json, hatch a new pet: a short, cute, made-up name, a random body
color, and a birthday of right now. Show a little hatching animation the
first time.

The pet has hunger, energy, and happiness, each from 0 to 100. Time keeps
passing while the program is not running. Use the real time since the
last run to catch up:
    every hour away: hunger +8, energy -4, happiness -3
    nothing goes below 0 or above 100

It keeps its own clock: between 22:00 and 07:00 it is asleep. Use the
zone in the TZ environment variable, or UTC if TZ is unset. A sleeping
pet ignores feed and play, snores softly ("z", "Z", "z" floating up),
and gains energy instead of losing it.

The first argument is the command:
    (none)  show the pet for 8 seconds, idling: blink, look around,
            sometimes hop. Underneath, three colored bars for hunger,
            energy, and happiness, and one line of what it's thinking,
            based on its mood ("tummy rumbles...", "zoomies!!").
    feed    hunger -30, an eating animation with crumbs falling
    play    happiness +25, energy -15, a bouncing animation with hearts
    sleep   energy +40, lights dim, it curls up
    anything else: print the list of commands and exit 1

Draw the pet with small hand-made ASCII art in its body color: about
5 lines tall, with different faces for happy, hungry, tired, and asleep.
Animate at about 10 frames per second, redrawing in place. Use 24-bit
color, because stdout is not a terminal.

After every command, add 1 to the pet's age in "visits", save pet.json,
and print one closing line like "Mochi is 3 days old, visit #12."

If hunger reaches 100, the pet runs away. Show a short sad animation,
write goodbye.txt next to pet.json with a letter from the pet (signed
with its name), delete pet.json, and exit 0. The next run hatches a new
pet.

Never crash on a damaged pet.json. Say the old pet wandered off, then
hatch a new one.
